  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nShowers continue to lift northward through western and south\ncentral ND ahead of an upper level low pressure system currently\ntracking through Wyoming. Occasional showers will continue\nmainly south and west of the Missouri River through the day \ntoday, although there was a bit of a northward shift in the \nprecipitation and light rain has pushed a little north of the \nMissouri in far northwest ND. Rain amounts here though will \nremain very light. As the day goes on, better precipitation \nchances will shift to the south. This evening precipitation is \nexpected to taper off over southwest and south central ND, west \nof the Missouri. Another cool day is expected today, especially\nin the southwest where highs are forecast to be in the mid 50s.\nWe should see clear to partly cloudy skies most areas late \ntonight into Monday morning, thus cooler overnight lows in the \n40s are forecast. \n\nOverall, it looks like a pretty quiet week ahead. We will see a\ngradual warming trend. Highs start out mainly in the 60s on \nMonday, the climb into the mid 70s to mid 80s by the end of the\nworkweek. One hiccup will be Wednesday through Wednesday night.\nAn upper level low, currently off the California coast lifts\nnortheast across the Rockies and into the Northern Plains by\nWednesday. This will bring a chance of showers and possibly a \nfew thunderstorms spreading southwest to northeast across the \nforecast area. Late in the workweek, we are situated between \nupper level high pressure over, or just east of us, with another\nupper level low to our west. Currently the NBM is depicting dry\nweather each day and can't argue with that, especially \nThursday. However, with temperatures creeping into the 80s \nwest, an isolated afternoon/evening thunderstorm Can't be ruled \nout Friday afternoon or evening. On Saturday the upper low to\nour west closes off and lifts northeast into Alberta and \nSaskatchewan, with the associated cold front pushing through the\narea Saturday-Sunday. Uncertainty is quite high at this time, \nbut another shot of showers and a few thunderstorms may be \npossible over the weekend. Temperature wise, highs in the 70s \nand 80s at the end of the workweek cool into the 60s to lower \n70s by Sunday.",
